# A record that lists no name has no slot. As a compiled object it holds extra properties alone, and each new key makes it a structure of its own: a bag of eight fresh keys took 21,030 instructions, and takes 15,285.
# So the objects of such a layout are ordinary objects where reached code fills one by key: `counts`, and `state.cache`, which lies in a record of data.
# What is stored under a name too has a field for the name and keeps its slots (`named`, `listed`: the front end gives both one layout). What nobody fills stays as it is (`empty`).
# The answer claims nothing, so a wrong one costs speed alone. It hangs on what either reading of Link knows of the subject of a store: an object that unseen code has had is known by its layout in what is held alone.
# With every mechanism withheld no layout holds ordinary objects.
# RUN(LINK): link layouts held mechanisms=all
# LINK: layout L0 {{*}} filled-under-computed-keys
# LINK-NEXT: layout L1 changes=[]
# LINK-NEXT: layout L2 {{*}} filled-under-computed-keys
# LINK-NEXT: layout L3 changes=[]
# LINK-NEXT: layout L4 {{*}} filled-under-computed-keys
# RUN: plan mechanisms=all
# CHECK: layout L0 OrdinaryObject
# CHECK: layout L1 CompiledObject
# CHECK: layout L2 OrdinaryObject
# CHECK: layout L3 CompiledObject
# CHECK: layout L4 CompiledObject
# RUN(WALK): walk f0 f1 f2 f4 mechanisms=all
# WALK-LABEL: code f0:Body
# WALK: [[cache]]: object?ObjectWithoutLayout @Cell = NewDictionary
# WALK: NewObject -, {{*}} layout=L3 fields=[0]
# WALK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# WALK-NOT: NewObject
# WALK: NewDictionary
# WALK-LABEL: code f2:Body
# WALK: NewObject layout=L4
# WALK-LABEL: code f4:Body
# WALK: NewObject layout=L1
# RUN(WITHHELD): plan mechanisms=none
# WITHHELD-NOT: OrdinaryObject
# WITHHELD: layout L0 CompiledObject
# WITHHELD: layout L2 CompiledObject
